    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c1f4e600-d3c4-52ee-82ac-17dc521b0351",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=1.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=1-(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=-1$ and $z=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)-(2)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$, $y=-1$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨- \\frac{1}{2},-1,2⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
