      ":question/id": "0eeb6dea-7a81-55f9-bd18-ccd1b16eefab",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=4 \\\\ y+2z &=2 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(\\frac{1}{2})=2.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=1$ and $z=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(1)-(\\frac{1}{2})=4.\n$$\n\n$$\nx=4-(-1)-(- \\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{11}{2}$, $y=1$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\n$$",
