    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2594c02e-f482-5003-b505-8c93d438c640",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-3(\\frac{1}{3})=2.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=2-(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+3(\\frac{1}{3})=9.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=9-(-3)-(1)=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\n$$",
