      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=- \\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2})+(-1)=2.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2-(-1)-(-1)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨4,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩.\n$$",
